        "heading": "2.1 HUMAN VISION ABILITIES",
        "text": "Static vision ability (SVA) and dynamic vision ability (DVA) are two basic functions of human visual acuity. Related research has discovered individual differences in DVA in subjects with a similar SVA \u2013 the low correlation between these two abilities causes researchers to measure them separately.\nStatic Vision Ability. For SVA research, minimum detectable threshold, minimum resolution threshold, minimum perceptible alignment threshold, and minimum recognition threshold are four basic thresholds in measurement (Chan & Courtney (1996)). Researchers usually use the above thresholds for experimental design, such as the widely used optotype for visual acuity tests is designed by the minimum recognition threshold. However, some researchers indicate only using SVA to assess the human visual system is limited (Long & Zavod (2002)). First, many visual stimuli in actual application scenes are often in motion. Besides, the SVA tests are displayed under conditions of maximum contrast (e.g., black symbols on white background), which is seldom observed in daily life.\nDynamic Vision Ability. For DVA research, researchers initially reference the SVA task and try to require subjects to observe moving objects on a high-contrast background. For example, an early work displays some moving plus (+) signs on the screen \u2013 subjects need to track the signs that have been flashing and to indicate whenever one of those targets flashed (Pylyshyn & Storm (1988)). Another DVA research avenue primarily serves athletes. Since several works have revealed that the DVA of humans is essential for their sports performance, a machine named Bernell\u2019s Rotator has been used in sports optometry. Recently, some researchers have tried to use computer programs to evaluate the DVA of athletes (Quevedo et al. (2012)). This evaluation mechanism includes various stimuli with different color and motion trajectories. Although the evaluation methods have developed from simple designs to comprehensive computer programs, they always have limited target categories (e.g., specific symbols), simple video scenes (e.g., colors or some static photographs), and limited motion patterns (e.g., lateral, vertical, oblique, and linear trajectories)."
